WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.164
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.464 --> 00:00:08.897
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.197 --> 00:00:16.099
Unit twenty-five: Pragmatism. Today you will judge ideas by results using insofar as / to the extent that.

4
00:00:16.399 --> 00:00:19.121
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.555
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:25.522
Pragmatism.

7
00:00:26.517 --> 00:00:31.767
Pragmatism means the philosophy that ideas are to be judged by their practical effects.

8
00:00:32.383 --> 00:00:36.008
Pragmatism began in the United States in the late nineteenth century.

9
00:00:36.617 --> 00:00:39.118
Your turn. Say: Pragmatism.

10
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:42.968
Pragmatism.

11
00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:44.590
Practical.

12
00:00:45.583 --> 00:00:49.069
Practical means concerned with action and results rather than theory.

13
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:52.588
A practical solution works in real conditions.

14
00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:55.673
Your turn. Say: Practical.

15
00:00:58.533 --> 00:00:59.442
Practical.

16
00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:01.347
Consequence.

17
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:05.174
Consequence means a result or effect of an action.

18
00:01:05.783 --> 00:01:09.536
Peirce urged us to consider the conceivable consequences of an idea.

19
00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.930
Your turn. Say: Consequence.

20
00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:16.977
Consequence.

21
00:01:17.717 --> 00:01:19.008
Experience.

22
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:23.073
Experience means knowledge gained through direct involvement.

23
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:26.217
For James, experience is the test of belief.

24
00:01:26.817 --> 00:01:29.724
Your turn. Say: Experience.

25
00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:33.823
Experience.

26
00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:35.271
Truth.

27
00:01:36.283 --> 00:01:40.210
Truth means agreement with reality, or what proves reliable in practice.

28
00:01:40.817 --> 00:01:43.675
For pragmatists, truth is what survives testing.

29
00:01:44.283 --> 00:01:46.912
Your turn. Say: Truth.

30
00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:50.628
Truth.

31
00:01:51.383 --> 00:01:52.280
Inquiry.

32
00:01:53.283 --> 00:01:57.036
Inquiry means the process of investigating a question to settle doubt.

33
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.195
Dewey described inquiry as problem-solving.

34
00:02:00.783 --> 00:02:03.076
Your turn. Say: Inquiry.

35
00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:06.911
Inquiry.

36
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:08.860
Usefulness.

37
00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:13.220
Usefulness means the quality of being helpful or effective.

38
00:02:13.817 --> 00:02:16.884
Usefulness is the pragmatist's measure of a concept.

39
00:02:17.483 --> 00:02:20.066
Your turn. Say: Usefulness.

40
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.238
Usefulness.

41
00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:26.159
Problem-solving.

42
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.914
Problem-solving means finding effective answers to difficulties.

43
00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:34.213
Problem-solving is how inquiry advances.

44
00:02:34.817 --> 00:02:37.968
Your turn. Say: Problem-solving.

45
00:02:40.833 --> 00:02:42.218
Problem-solving.

46
00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:46.754
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.617 --> 00:02:52.521
Visal, do pragmatists think that anything useful is true? That seems dangerous.

48
00:02:53.617 --> 00:03:01.180
Not quite. An idea is true insofar as it works, meaning that it survives testing over time.

49
00:03:02.283 --> 00:03:04.411
But a lie can work in the short term.

50
00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:18.037
Exactly why pragmatists stress long-run inquiry. To the extent that a belief withstands criticism and experience, it earns the label true.

51
00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:22.113
So truth is a process, not a snapshot?

52
00:03:23.217 --> 00:03:29.793
Precisely. Insofar as circumstances change, our best beliefs must be revised.

53
00:03:30.933 --> 00:03:32.318
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:33.183 --> 00:03:36.634
Grammar focus: Insofar as and to the extent that.

55
00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:38.202
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.917 --> 00:03:41.613
An idea is true insofar as it works.

57
00:03:42.517 --> 00:03:46.478
A belief earns the label true to the extent that it withstands criticism.

58
00:03:47.383 --> 00:03:51.333
Our beliefs must be revised insofar as circumstances change.

59
00:03:52.300 --> 00:04:02.722
Insofar as / in so far as / to the extent that = only to the degree that. A full clause follows. They limit a claim: true insofar as it works (not 'insofar it works').

60
00:04:03.483 --> 00:04:07.738
Be careful! Don't say: An idea is true in so far it works.

61
00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:13.197
Say: An idea is true insofar as it works.

62
00:04:13.497 --> 00:04:15.346
insofar as + clause.

63
00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:18.751
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:20.928
Truth in Practice.

65
00:04:21.228 --> 00:04:28.684
Pragmatism, developed by Peirce, James and Dewey, holds that the meaning of an idea lies in its practical consequences.

66
00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:38.201
James proposed that an idea is true insofar as it proves reliable in guiding action, which many critics mistook for the claim that whatever is convenient is true.

67
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:47.039
Defenders respond that usefulness is judged over the long run, through inquiry open to correction, and that lies seldom survive such scrutiny.

68
00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:53.818
Dewey extended the view to democracy, treating social problems as tasks for experimental problem-solving.

69
00:04:54.833 --> 00:05:00.420
To the extent that its tools prove effective, pragmatism has influenced education, law and science.

70
00:05:01.533 --> 00:05:05.651
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:06.517 --> 00:05:08.783
An idea is true blank it works.

72
00:05:09.083 --> 00:05:13.538
A, insofar as. B, insofar. C, so far of.

73
00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.726
The answer is A: insofar as.

74
00:05:20.883 --> 00:05:24.276
The pragmatist measure of a concept is its practical blank.

75
00:05:24.576 --> 00:05:28.578
A, essence. B, consequences. C, paradox.

76
00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:34.516
The answer is B: consequences.

77
00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:37.928
Dewey described inquiry as blank.

78
00:05:38.228 --> 00:05:42.462
A, problem-solving. B, bad faith. C, metaphysics.

79
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.139
The answer is A: problem-solving.

80
00:05:49.283 --> 00:05:51.086
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:51.386 --> 00:05:59.950
A, It is valid to the extent that it helps.. B, It is valid to extent that helps.. C, It is valid to the extent it that helps..

82
00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.939
The answer is A: It is valid to the extent that it helps..

83
00:06:08.083 --> 00:06:11.348
According to the reading, pragmatists judge usefulness…

84
00:06:11.648 --> 00:06:16.126
A, in the short run only. B, over the long run. C, never.

85
00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:21.956
The answer is B: over the long run.

86
00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:26.283
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.533
Should ideas be judged by their results?

88
00:06:46.317 --> 00:06:47.898
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:48.198 --> 00:07:15.936
Ideas should be judged by their results, but with care. An idea is worth keeping insofar as it helps us solve problems and survives honest testing. Short-term convenience is not enough, so results must be examined over time and by many people. Some values, such as justice, may not reduce to outcomes. Pragmatism is a valuable corrective to empty theory without being the whole story.

90
00:07:16.236 --> 00:07:17.969
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:18.883 --> 00:07:23.869
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and insofar as and to the extent that.

92
00:07:24.169 --> 00:07:29.028
Today's value is practical wisdom. Judge ideas by how well they work in real life.

93
00:07:29.328 --> 00:07:32.340
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.685
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

